@page "/ribbon"

<CatHead Title="Ribbons">
    A word on the corner of a <a href="/card">card</a> or a tile &mdash; &ldquo;New&rdquo;,
    &ldquo;Beta&rdquo;, &ldquo;Retiring&rdquo; &mdash; that says something about the whole thing it
    sits on. A state of one value is a <a href="/badge">badge</a>; a state that needs a sentence is an
    <a href="/alert">alert</a>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Ribbon.Band" Title="Band across the corner" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.ribbon</code> inside a <code>.ribbon-host</code>, which clips it at its own edge so the
        card's radius still shapes the corner. Keep it to one or two words.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Ribbon.Tones" Title="Tones" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A ribbon takes the library's tones, as a <a href="/badge">badge</a> does: brand by default,
        then <code>--go</code>, <code>--warn</code>, <code>--danger</code>, <code>--info</code> and
        <code>--secret</code> for what the word means, <code>--cyan</code>, <code>--orange</code> and
        <code>--teal</code> for a hue that means nothing, and <code>--muted</code> for a word that asks
        nothing of the reader. A ribbon is filled by nature, so the tone alone gives the fill, in the
        band and the tab alike.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Ribbon.Sizes" Title="Sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>--sm</code> for a small tile, the default, and <code>--lg</code> for a hero card. The
        sizes are the same on the band and the tab; the word stays one or two words at every size.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Ribbon.Tab" Title="Tab in the corner" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.ribbon--tab</code> is the quieter shape, a flat tab tucked into the corner, for a grid
        where several tiles carry one.
    </p>
</CatExample>
